r-2kyztz" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="9ea05f3c-4b7c-49d1-800a-99336c41fff8" aria-controls="replo-:R3rr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1iunbf2"><div data-rid="6c1fb2d0-3e83-4c37-9b03-f2c8eb17edd4" class="r-l2nutd alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><h3>Can I take whey protein if I'm lactose intolerant?</h3></span></div><div data-rid="99e5a0e6-72bd-499a-9eff-af91a4a36969" class="r-fcc2w7"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-1izeoa" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R3rr2:"><div data-rid="3e5cec72-fd36-40ba-9986-acbc6d494799" class="r-14quc7z"><div data-rid="ad5ac6af-5e08-4446-ba28-ec9ca0db8734" class="r-1mltz0n alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Whey protein isolate, which contains very little lactose, is often well-tolerated by individuals with lactose intolerance. However, those with severe lactose intolerance may still experience discomfort and should opt for lactose-free whey protein alternatives or consult with a healthcare professional.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="91876f13-6c99-4a3a-a1db-58eb2858360c" class="r-1bpz9d1"></div><div data-rid="4b50a1d4-e7c1-4375-b3ef-df7ce813105b" class="r-1hjh2i8" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="61b09bc8-820c-4307-8b84-af06efe0cbb0" aria-controls="replo-:R5rr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-4yk9os"><div data-rid="4bd4ba99-f247-4100-9e70-1ce33fdca30c" class="r-8p7vn9 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Is whey protein only for athletes and bodybuilders?</p></span></div><div data-rid="2cb09dd5-00e5-444e-93c0-db1d778e6069" class="r-f6v31z"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-bw6eku" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R5rr2:"><div data-rid="a041a3b2-0ca0-4611-bcfe-6d44be7165c4" class="r-5n6u8y"><div data-rid="327b6084-0535-4532-b5c5-f8efdff5148c" class="r-14kzite alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>No, whey protein can benefit anyone looking to increase their protein intake, including individuals pursuing weight loss, looking to maintain muscle mass, and individuals with busy lifestyles seeking convenient nutrition options. It's a versatile supplement that can support various health and fitness goals.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="40187798-cd73-47db-a6c2-5bf5ac7cea6d" class="r-18szeci"></div><div data-rid="ce63b815-50c6-4ba2-9e16-edf4d25f63b1" class="r-1ry0u29" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="748981de-238b-4e32-b876-fd83654ca3be" aria-controls="replo-:R7rr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-wte0mj"><div data-rid="609f46ee-271c-4ab3-a711-46e59699ac2e" class="r-1pfijc8 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Can I use whey protein for weight loss?</p></span></div><div data-rid="237d57ed-bb6c-450d-bc52-424fce4ad54d" class="r-wwogqn"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-32kgi7" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R7rr2:"><div data-rid="a7f64b56-bca8-4dde-a5e8-1fc70d80dfcd" class="r-msefw0"><div data-rid="c779cfde-74e9-4cb6-a96c-ad655e0c5364" class="r-1rfavy alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Yes, whey protein can be a helpful component of a weight loss plan. It can help increase feelings of fullness, support muscle maintenance during calorie restriction, and aid in fat loss when combined with a balanced diet and consistent exercise.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="fdc5d481-973d-4745-b3bc-1cb55e64fd08" class="r-84x5on"></div><div data-rid="a5936f85-8ef6-4c18-bfeb-83b6f5cbd6f8" class="r-gppmgj" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="6a618c27-fbc2-4f40-ad92-42f86882be56" aria-controls="replo-:R9rr2:" tabindex="0" role="button" class="r-1rbs45z"><div data-rid="75e7ff35-698e-4698-aee9-90b985a76382" class="r-xgl8a4 alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Does whey protein have any side effects?</p></span></div><div data-rid="f52ed3a6-62a9-4b67-98d2-ba6f4ad83f3e" class="r-qvdwnf"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="Collapse/Expand" style="width:var(--rsw, 100%);height:100%" class="r-4gmjom" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M8 4a.5.5 0 0 1 .5.5v3h3a.5.5 0 0 1 0 1h-3v3a.5.5 0 0 1-1 0v-3h-3a.5.5 0 0 1 0-1h3v-3A.5.5 0 0 1 8 4"></path></svg></div></div><div hidden="" id="replo-:R9rr2:"><div data-rid="ed06d283-ebec-411d-85f7-56ec77efe1f4" class="r-3yklcd"><div data-rid="0635cb29-0b07-4279-b3f4-4b4ae5531e53" class="r-18pbxlj alchemy-rte"><span style="width:100%"><p>Whey protein has no side effects  when consumed moderately . However, excessive intake may lead to digestive issues such as bloating, gas, or diarrhea. It's essential to follow the recommended serving size and consult with a healthcare professional if you have concerns.</p></span></div></div></div></div><div data-rid="a0b7d723-f48e-4931-9d99-1a0629de789a" class="r-yqho2c"></div><div data-rid="8b3732bb-7f7c-4fce-8187-5e271a53a6b0" class="r-or0wyy" data-replo-collapsible="true" data-state="closed"><div data-rid="07bc7a5f-a8